isable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\n- Producer: SANY: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\n- refinement: SANY: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\n\n- module BoundedQueue :: init: ERROR\n    note: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\nFirst error line: Parsing or semantic analysis failed.\n- module BoundedQueue :: consec: ERROR\n    note: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\nFirst error line: Parsing or semantic analysis failed.\n- module BoundedQueue :: property: ERROR\n    note: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\n- module Producer :: init: ERROR\n    note: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\n- module Producer :: consec: ERROR\n    note: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\n- module Producer :: property: ERROR\n    note: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\n- refinement obligation: ERROR\n    note: TLC exited with a non-zero code but no recognisable invariant or initial-predicate violation was found in its output. This usually means a parse/semantic error in the spec or config.\nFirst error line: Parsing or semantic analysis failed.\n\nLast bundle slug: producer_consumer_system\nParent module: ProducerConsumerSystem\nModules: BoundedQueue(abs), BoundedQueue(impl), Producer(abs), Producer(impl), Consumer(abs), Consumer(impl)\nRevise the failing module(s) and/or abstraction map(s). Return one call to repair_module_bundle with the FULL revised bundle."}]}], 'model': 'claude-opus-4-7', 'system': [{'type': 'text', 'text': 'You are repairing a multi-module ModuleBundle that failed one or more proof\nobligations under TLC. The user message lists each failing obligation: it\nmay be a per-impl (Init/Consec/Property) failure, or the cross-module\nrefinement obligation.\n\nYour task\n---------\nDiagnose the failures and emit a revised bundle via the\n`repair_module_bundle` tool. Set `targeted_failure` to point at the most\nimportant failure (e.g. `"Queue/consec"`, `"refinement"`, `"parent/property"`).\nProvide a brief `reasoning`.\n\nDiagnosis guidance\n------------------\n* Per-impl `init`/`consec`/`property` failures behave exactly like the\n  single-module flow: weaken or strengthen `Inv`, or fix the algorithm.\n* `refinement` failure: the impl\'s abstraction_map is wrong (e.g. it\n  projects onto a state that does NOT correspond to a valid Abs state) OR\n  the impl does something the Abs cannot simulate. Either fix the map or\n  restrict the impl.\n* Parent failures: the parent\'s composed `Inv` does not hold, or fails to\n  imply the parent\'s `Property`. Strengthen the composed `Inv`, possibly\n  pulling in an additional impl invariant via INSTANCE projection.\n\nOutput rules\n------------\nRe-emit the WHOLE bundle (parent + every module), even unchanged ones, so\nthe verif